 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 542301,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Three students have been asked to determine if Graphs <i>E</i> and <i>T</i> are isomorphic and justify their answers.\n<br>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>Graph <i>E</i> and Graph <i>T</i></figcaption>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/a4808ca1eaf87766.png\" alt=\"Two graphs are labeled graph E and graph T. Graph E has four vertices: a, b, c, and d. The edges connect a b, b c, a d, and c d. Graph T has four vertices: p, q, r, and s. The edges connect p q, q s, s r, and r p.\">\n</figure>\n<br>\nJavier believes that Graphs <i>E</i> and <i>T</i> are not isomorphic because Graph <i>E</i> contains a triangle cycle and Graph <i>T</i> does not. He supports his conclusion with the graphs shown.\n<figure>\n<figcaption>Javier’s Highlighted Cycle</figcaption>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/f8fcb9a9d56b09b7.png\" alt=\"Two graphs are labeled graph E and graph T. Graph E has four vertices: a, b, c, and d. The edges connect a b, b c, a d, and c d. The upper triangle formed by the edges in the graph is highlighted. Graph T has four vertices: p, q, r, and s. The edges connect p q, q s, s r, and r p.\">\n</figure>\nMaubi also believes that Graphs <i>E</i> and <i>T</i> are not isomorphic, but says that it is because Graph <i>T</i> has a quadrilateral cycle (<i>p</i>, <i>q</i>, <i>r</i>, <i>s</i>) while Graph <i>E</i> does not. Caden believes that Graphs <i>E</i> and <i>T</i> are isomorphic. She said one isomorphism is <i>a-p</i>, <i>b-q</i>, <i>c-s</i>, and <i>d-r</i>. Determine who is correct, who is incorrect, and explain how you know.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Be careful you do not do what Javier did. Javier highlighted a crossover where there is no vertex. There is no cyclic subgraph in graph E.</p>\n<p>Maubi is incorrect because you can trace your finger around Graph E from vertex <i>a</i> back to <i>a</i>. Graph E is a quadrilateral, a four-sided cycle (<i>a, b, c, d</i>). Graph T also is a four-sided cycle (<i>p, q, r, s</i>).</p>\n<p>Both graphs have 4 vertices, 4 edges, and no cyclic subgraphs. All the vertices have degree 2.</p>\n<p>By flipping vertex <i>d</i> around <i>c</i>, you can transform Graph E into visual facsimile of Graph T.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Caden is correct because Graph <i>E</i> could be untangled so that <i>d</i> is to the left of <i>c</i> and the edges <i>bc</i> and <i>ad</i> are no longer crossed. Maubi is incorrect because (<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, <i>c</i>, <i>d</i>) is a quadrilateral in Graph <i>E</i>. Javier is incorrect because the portion of the graph he highlighted does not have 3 vertices and is not a triangle."
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
